#!/usr/bin/env bash
# roost-codex-hook — report an OpenAI Codex CLI agent's state onto its tmux pane.
#
# Install by printing the registration and trusting it once:
#
#   roost hooks codex          # prints the JSON, with the real path for your copy
#   # write the JSON object into ~/.codex/hooks.json, then:
#   codex                      # answer "Trust all and continue" at "Hooks need review"
#
# `roost doctor` prints both steps with the real paths, and checks that the
# second one was actually done — see the T6 note below for why it has to.
#
# --- why this file exists at all ---------------------------------------------
#
# It maps four codex hook events onto `roost state` calls, and it makes ONE
# decision of its own: whether a Stop is `done` or `error`. Codex has no error
# event, so this file reads the Stop payload itself and badges a Stop that
# carries no reply `error` (#39) — see "Stop: a finished turn, or a dead one"
# below, which is where that logic lives and where to change it.
#
# scripts/roost-agent-state — Claude Code's hook — does every other hard part:
# codex's Stop payload spells the reply field `last_assistant_message`, exactly
# as Claude Code spells it (verified live on codex-cli 0.150.1 and again on
# 0.151.0), so that script's JSON reader parses a codex payload with no change
# at all. What it gained for codex is small and named where it sits: an
# `error --stop-hook` arm that clears the previous reply, and
# @roost-error-reason.
#
# The obvious question is then why there is a file here instead of four
# hooks.json commands pointing straight at scripts/roost-agent-state. The answer
# is codex's registration model, and it is the whole design (spec §4):
#
#   Codex stores a hash of each normalised handler object in
#   $CODEX_HOME/config.toml and SKIPS any handler whose hash no longer matches.
#   Not warns — skips, with nothing printed on stdout, on stderr, or in the TUI.
#
# Measured on 0.150.1, both directions: appending one argument to a command
# string took 8 of 8 hooks down while the turn still succeeded; changing a
# single `timeout` from 10 to 11 took 7 of 8 down. Rewriting the SCRIPT the
# command points at changed nothing — all four kept firing, running the new
# code, with no re-prompt.
#
# So the four command strings roost emits are frozen forever, and this file is
# where roost is allowed to change its mind. Wiring hooks.json directly at
# roost-agent-state would have spent that one-shot budget on an argument list
# ("done --stop-hook") that could then never be revised.
#
# Two rules fall out of that and neither is optional:
#
#   - The event name is the ONLY argument. Everything else — which state, which
#     flags, whether an event is used at all — is decided here, where it can
#     still be changed.
#   - Never tune the timeout in `roost hooks codex`. Pick one, keep it. To a
#     user, a changed timeout and a changed command are the same silent
#     breakage.
#
# Adding a NEW event to hooks.json later is safe: measured on 0.151.0, a
# four-event file was trusted, a fifth event was added, and the four already
# trusted kept firing on the next run (the new one did not, until trusted).
# Growing the registration is allowed; editing it is not.
#
# --- T6: untrusted is completely silent --------------------------------------
#
# Until a human answers "Trust all and continue", every hook here is skipped and
# codex says nothing about it. This is the whole, unedited stderr of a
# `codex exec` turn with untrusted hooks wired: warnings about hook timeouts,
# a warning about model metadata, and not one word about the hooks being
# skipped. Codex's own docs promise a startup warning pointing at `/hooks` —
# true in the TUI, false in `codex exec`, which is where automation lives.
# `roost doctor` therefore CHECKS for the trust entries rather than inferring
# them from hooks.json being in place.
#
# --- what is NOT here --------------------------------------------------------
#
# An error EVENT. Codex has exactly twelve hook events — PreToolUse
# PermissionRequest PostToolUse PreCompact PostCompact SessionStart SessionEnd
# UserPromptSubmit SubagentStart SubagentStop Stop Interrupt — and none of them
# reports an error. A turn that dies on the provider still fires Stop, and until
# #39 a codex pane badged that dead turn ✅ done.
#
# `error` is now reached anyway, by an INFERENCE from a field codex really
# sends, and the inference is announced rather than hidden: docs/known-gaps.md
# and the Codex section of site/content/docs/state-badges.md both name it and
# its limits. A dead turn's Stop carries no `last_assistant_message`, so a Stop
# whose payload parses and holds no reply text badges 💥 error — see the Stop
# arm below for what that does and does not catch.
#
# The heuristic that was rejected is still rejected: an unmatched PreToolUse.
# A live turn whose tool call failed showed five PreToolUse against four
# PostToolUse, so it mislabels a healthy turn `error` and pings the human's
# desktop for nothing (spec §1). "No reply at all" has no such healthy case
# that anyone has measured — which is exactly the claim known-gaps keeps open.
#
# `idle`, and this one is a measured trap rather than a missing feature. Mapping
# SessionEnd -> idle reads correctly in a TUI, where SessionEnd fires only when
# codex exits — a two-turn TUI session fired Stop twice and SessionEnd once, on
# ^C. Under `codex exec` it is a different event: SessionEnd lands 22ms, 25ms
# and 40ms after Stop in three separate captures, in the same process. It would
# overwrite the ✅ that turn just earned with 💤 a frame later, and 💤 is the
# badge that says "nothing here to look at". spec §1 settles it from the other
# side too: idle is a DEFAULT, not a report — an unstamped pane already renders
# as idle, and neither shipped adapter has ever reported it.
#
# `SubagentStop`. A child's end is announced on its own event, which is what
# keeps Stop meaning the pane's own turn (spec §5 T1). Not registering it is the
# filter.
set -u

# The event name codex was told to pass, and the only thing it passes.
event="${1:-}"

# The mapping table. Every arm was observed firing on this machine against
# codex-cli 0.150.1 and 0.151.0; nothing here comes from a schema.
#
#   UserPromptSubmit  a turn starts                       -> working
#   PostToolUse       a tool returned, so the turn is live -> working
#   PermissionRequest a human is being waited on          -> blocked
#   Stop              the turn ended                      -> done + the reply
#   Stop, no reply    the turn ended with nothing to say  -> error + why (#39)
#
# PostToolUse is doing two jobs, and the second is the important one: it is what
# CLEARS 🛑. No hook fires when the human answers a permission dialog, so
# PostToolUse is the first observable event afterwards — measured on 0.151.0,
# one second after "1. Yes, proceed", with the dialog on screen until then. The
# order is PreToolUse -> PermissionRequest -> (human) -> PostToolUse, so the
# badge goes working then blocked, never the reverse.
#
# PreToolUse is deliberately not registered even though it is the natural
# partner of PostToolUse: UserPromptSubmit has already reported `working` by
# then, so it would spend a process spawn per tool call to report a state the
# pane is already in.
#
# Anything else exits 0 having done nothing. roost registers four events, so
# codex never invokes this with another name — but a user is free to add one to
# their own hooks.json, and a badge from a signal nobody measured is worse than
# no badge.
stop_hook=0
case "$event" in
  UserPromptSubmit|PostToolUse) state="working" ;;
  PermissionRequest)            state="blocked" ;;
  Stop)                         state="done"; stop_hook=1 ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# --- Stop: a finished turn, or a dead one (#39) -------------------------------
#
# A turn that never reached its model still fires Stop, and what gives it away
# is what that Stop does NOT carry: known-gaps records `last_assistant_message`
# as empty for a dead turn. So Stop is split three ways on that one field:
#
#   parses, and the field is a non-empty string  -> done   (a turn that answered)
#   parses, and the field is "", null or absent  -> error  (a turn that said nothing)
#   does not parse, or nothing here can parse it -> done   (we cannot tell)
#
# All three spellings of "nothing" are treated alike because only "empty" was
# ever recorded, not the raw bytes, and a reader keyed on one spelling would
# pass its own test and miss the other two.
#
# The third arm is the honest limit, not an oversight. On a machine with neither
# python3 nor jq nothing is known about ANY turn, and badging every one of them
# error would fire a desktop notification per turn — the "no badge beats a
# wrong badge" rule applied to the whole machine. It keeps the pre-#39
# behaviour, and docs/known-gaps.md names it as still uncovered.
#
# This is an inference, and it is announced as one: known-gaps and the Codex
# section of site/content/docs/state-badges.md both say a codex 💥 means "the
# turn ended with no reply", not "codex reported an error".
#
# The payload is read HERE, once, and handed on to roost-agent-state on its
# stdin, because both need it and a pipe can only be read once. Under a tty (a
# human running this by hand) it is not read at all — `cat` would wait for ^D —
# and the sink is called exactly as before, which skips its own read the same
# way.
#
# Not outside tmux, either. ~/.codex/hooks.json is GLOBAL, so this runs on every
# codex turn on the machine, and the sink's own guard — no $TMUX or no
# $TMUX_PANE, exit 0 — only fires after this block. Parsing first spent a
# python3 spawn per turn on codex runs that roost will never badge (on a Mac
# without the Command Line Tools, the /usr/bin/python3 stub). The same two
# tests the sink makes, so this costs no process; the finer roost-socket check
# stays in the sink, where lib/roost-socket.sh keeps the one copy of that rule.
payload=""
reason=""
if [ "$stop_hook" = 1 ] && [ ! -t 0 ] && [ -n "${TMUX:-}" ] && [ -n "${TMUX_PANE:-}" ]; then
  payload="$(cat 2>/dev/null || true)"
  verdict=""
  # The same reader preference as roost-agent-state, for the same reason: a
  # hand-rolled JSON parse in shell fails subtly, and neither reader may be
  # required. `isinstance(d, dict)` because a payload that is valid JSON but
  # not an object is as unreadable as one that is not JSON at all.
  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    verdict="$(printf '%s' "$payload" | python3 -c '
import sys, json
d = json.load(sys.stdin)
if isinstance(d, dict):
    m = d.get("last_assistant_message")
    sys.stdout.write("done" if isinstance(m, str) and m else "error")
' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  elif command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    verdict="$(printf '%s' "$payload" | jq -r '
      if type == "object" then
        (if (.last_assistant_message | type) == "string" and .last_assistant_message != ""
         then "done" else "error" end)
      else empty end' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  if [ "$verdict" = "error" ]; then
    state="error"
    # A fixed sentence, not payload text: `roost wait-done` prints it verbatim,
    # and it must say what roost inferred and from what, so nobody reads it as
    # codex's own diagnosis. "no reply" is the phrase tests/test-codex-hook.sh
    # holds it to.
    reason="codex ended the turn with no reply (its Stop hook carried no last_assistant_message), which most often means the turn never reached the model — look at the pane"
  fi
fi

# Resolve this script's own directory even when invoked via a symlink — the same
# readlink loop as bin/roost and scripts/roost-agent-state. hooks.json holds an
# ABSOLUTE path to this file, and that path can perfectly well be a symlink (a
# checkout aliased somewhere stable so the frozen registration survives a move).
# Through one, `dirname "$0"` names the symlink's directory, and the sibling
# lookup below would miss — silently, because the call carries `|| true` and
# must keep carrying it.
SOURCE="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
while [ -L "$SOURCE" ]; do
  DIR="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$SOURCE")" && pwd)"
  SOURCE="$(readlink "$SOURCE")"
  [[ "$SOURCE" != /* ]] && SOURCE="$DIR/$SOURCE"
done
SELF_DIR="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$SOURCE")" && pwd)"

# ROOST_AGENT_NAME, or every codex pane's border and switcher row reads
# "claude": scripts/roost-agent-state's fallback is @roost-name-default, whose
# honest built-in value is "claude" because that script IS Claude Code's hook
# (spec §5 T9). A name the human chose still outranks this.
#
# --stop-hook only on Stop, and only Stop carries a reply. With `done` it makes
# roost-agent-state read the payload on stdin — the copy this script read from
# codex and re-feeds below — and stamp @roost-reply BEFORE @agent_state, so a
# caller following the documented `roost wait-done` then `roost read` idiom
# cannot land in the gap between the two (spec §2). With `error` it reads
# nothing and clears @roost-reply instead, because a dead turn has no answer.
#
# `|| true` and the bare `exit 0` are the same rule stated twice, and it is not
# defensive padding here the way it would be in a plugin. Codex READS this
# script's exit code: its own error strings name the contract — "UserPromptSubmit
# hook exited with code 2 but did not write a blocking reason to stderr",
# "PreToolUse hook returned unsupported continue:false". A non-zero exit out of a
# status-bar adapter is a veto on the user's tool call. So a missing sink, a dead
# tmux server or a pane that went away must leave the agent working and the pane
# unbadged, which is spec's "it must never throw" applied to a process.
#
# This is why the script is not `exec`ed: through `exec` there is no frame left
# to convert a failure into a 0, and a checkout moved after hooks.json was
# written would exit 126 straight into codex.
#
# stderr is deliberately NOT swallowed. Every tmux call in roost-agent-state is
# already quiet by construction, so anything that reaches stderr from here is a
# real fault (a missing file, an unreadable script) — and it is the only
# diagnostic a user will ever get, since codex only acts on a hook's stderr when
# the exit code is 2, which this never returns.
#
# On Stop the payload was already read above, so it is re-fed with printf
# rather than inherited — except under a tty, where nothing was read and the
# sink inherits the tty and skips its own read, as it always has. `error
# --stop-hook` makes the sink clear the previous turn's reply rather than record
# one; ROOST_ERROR_REASON is the sentence `roost wait-done` prints.
sink="$SELF_DIR/../../scripts/roost-agent-state"
if [ "$stop_hook" = 1 ] && [ ! -t 0 ]; then
  printf '%s' "$payload" \
    | ROOST_AGENT_NAME=codex ROOST_ERROR_REASON="$reason" "$sink" "$state" --stop-hook || true
elif [ "$stop_hook" = 1 ]; then
  ROOST_AGENT_NAME=codex "$sink" "$state" --stop-hook || true
else
  ROOST_AGENT_NAME=codex "$sink" "$state" || true
fi
exit 0
